WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.148
This part is about the vocabulary

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:06.774
of the course: the words we use for data.

00:00:06.774 --> 00:00:09.708
A type is a collection of values.

00:00:09.708 --> 00:00:12.557
The Boolean type has two values

00:00:12.557 --> 00:00:13.938
true and false

00:00:13.938 --> 00:00:16.700
the integers form another type.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.938
A simple type has values with no parts

00:00:19.938 --> 00:00:21.197
like an integer.

00:00:21.197 --> 00:00:23.196
A composite, or aggregate

00:00:23.196 --> 00:00:25.492
type has values made of parts

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:28.158
a bank account record holds a name

00:00:28.158 --> 00:00:31.490
an address, an account number and a balance.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.927
A data item is a piece of information

00:00:35.011 --> 00:00:37.764
whose value is drawn from a type

00:00:37.764 --> 00:00:39.683
a member of that type.

00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:42.687
A data type is a type together with

00:00:42.771 --> 00:00:45.357
the operations that work on it.

00:00:45.357 --> 00:00:48.194
A sequence of integers is a type

00:00:48.278 --> 00:00:50.531
a sequence of integers that

00:00:50.614 --> 00:00:52.116
you can append to

00:00:52.116 --> 00:00:55.120
index into and sort is a data type.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.708
Every data type has two forms.

00:00:57.708 --> 00:01:00.051
Its logical form is what it means

00:01:00.118 --> 00:01:02.060
the values and the operations

00:01:02.127 --> 00:01:03.934
as the user thinks of them.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:06.076
That is the abstract data type.

00:01:06.076 --> 00:01:08.687
Its physical form is how it is stored

00:01:08.687 --> 00:01:10.026
the bits in memory

00:01:10.093 --> 00:01:11.767
and the code that carries

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:13.106
out the operations.

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:14.980
That is the data structure.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.321
When you implement an ADT

00:01:17.321 --> 00:01:19.848
you work with its physical form

00:01:19.848 --> 00:01:23.064
when you use it elsewhere in the program

00:01:23.064 --> 00:01:26.050
you think only about its logical form.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.287
Java's int shows the gap.

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:30.400
Logically it is an integer.

00:01:30.475 --> 00:01:32.513
Physically it is 32

00:01:32.589 --> 00:01:34.475
bits in two's complement

00:01:34.475 --> 00:01:37.042
from -2^31

00:01:37.042 --> 00:01:40.060
up to 2^31 - 1.

00:01:40.136 --> 00:01:42.626
So the largest int + 1 wraps

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:44.438
around to the smallest.

00:01:44.513 --> 00:01:47.306
-5 is stored as a long row of

00:01:47.381 --> 00:01:49.796
ones ending in 1011.

00:01:49.872 --> 00:01:52.740
And 0.1 + 0.2

00:01:52.815 --> 00:01:55.230
is not exactly 0.3

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.400
because a double stores binary fractions.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.661
Neither surprise exists in the logical form

00:02:01.661 --> 00:02:03.910
both come from the physical form.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.036
Data abstraction means showing only

00:02:07.109 --> 00:02:09.364
the essential features of data

00:02:09.436 --> 00:02:11.255
and hiding the details of

00:02:11.328 --> 00:02:12.928
how it is implemented.

00:02:12.928 --> 00:02:14.601
Think of a smartphone.

00:02:14.674 --> 00:02:16.929
You know how to make a call and

00:02:17.001 --> 00:02:18.747
how to open the browser.

00:02:18.747 --> 00:02:21.438
You do not need to know how the CPU

00:02:21.511 --> 00:02:23.330
the battery and the radio

00:02:23.402 --> 00:02:24.930
make the call happen.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.623
A bank account is the same.

00:02:27.623 --> 00:02:30.833
It offers three operations: deposit

00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:33.176
withdraw, and get balance.

00:02:33.263 --> 00:02:36.734
You can use them without knowing whether

00:02:36.821 --> 00:02:39.250
the balance lives in memory

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.680
in a file or in a database.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:42.658
In Java

00:02:42.658 --> 00:02:46.110
abstraction comes from classes and objects

00:02:46.188 --> 00:02:48.071
from abstract data types

00:02:48.149 --> 00:02:49.875
written as interfaces

00:02:49.875 --> 00:02:51.758
and from encapsulation

00:02:51.837 --> 00:02:54.347
private fields that code outside

00:02:54.426 --> 00:02:56.230
the class cannot touch.

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.863
Here is the bank account

00:02:58.948 --> 00:03:01.514
as a Java interface: the what.

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:04.423
Three methods, deposit, withdraw

00:03:04.423 --> 00:03:05.877
and get balance

00:03:05.877 --> 00:03:08.699
each with its rule as a comment

00:03:08.699 --> 00:03:10.410
and no code at all.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.372
Two classes implement the interface

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.367
in different ways: two versions of the how.

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:18.682
Simple account stores one number

00:03:18.682 --> 00:03:20.111
the current balance.

00:03:20.111 --> 00:03:22.902
Ledger account stores the whole history

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:25.352
every deposit and every withdrawal

00:03:25.352 --> 00:03:27.190
and adds it up when asked.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.771
A client deposits 100

00:03:29.771 --> 00:03:32.597
withdraws 30, and deposits 50.

00:03:32.597 --> 00:03:33.861
Now look inside.

00:03:33.861 --> 00:03:37.208
Simple account holds 120.

00:03:37.208 --> 00:03:39.885
Ledger account holds three changes

00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:42.860
which add up to 120.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.769
Then the client tries to

00:03:44.834 --> 00:03:46.263
withdraw 500.

00:03:46.263 --> 00:03:47.692
Both accounts refuse

00:03:47.757 --> 00:03:49.315
and the balance is still

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:50.874
120.

00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:52.433
The client code and the

00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:53.862
output are identical

00:03:53.862 --> 00:03:56.330
the client cannot tell the two apart.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.355
And because balance is private

00:03:59.441 --> 00:04:01.857
no client can break the rule

00:04:01.943 --> 00:04:04.014
by writing to the field.

00:04:04.101 --> 00:04:07.034
Setting a.balance to 1,000,000

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:08.760
is a compile error.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.410
The slides list the benefits.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:12.266
Modularity

00:04:12.266 --> 00:04:15.121
users work with a few clear operations

00:04:15.193 --> 00:04:17.191
which makes programs simpler

00:04:17.263 --> 00:04:18.904
and easier to maintain.

00:04:18.904 --> 00:04:20.260
Data independence

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.473
the implementation can change

00:04:22.473 --> 00:04:25.257
from simple account to ledger account

00:04:25.257 --> 00:04:27.398
or from a file to a database

00:04:27.398 --> 00:04:29.468
without affecting the users.

00:04:29.468 --> 00:04:31.466
And security and integrity

00:04:31.466 --> 00:04:33.108
the data is protected

00:04:33.108 --> 00:04:35.820
so nobody can set a negative balance.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:38.247
So here is a question.

00:04:38.341 --> 00:04:41.645
A bank switches every customer from

00:04:41.739 --> 00:04:44.854
simple account to ledger account.

00:04:44.854 --> 00:04:49.007
Which lines of the bank client must change?

00:04:49.007 --> 00:04:51.650
Pause the video and decide.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.482
Only one: the new SimpleAccount() in main

00:04:58.482 --> 00:05:01.020
which picks the implementation.

00:05:01.099 --> 00:05:03.478
useAccount is written against

00:05:03.558 --> 00:05:06.889
the interface, and does not change at all.

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:09.030
That is data independence.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:12.227
Now the central idea of the course.

00:05:12.227 --> 00:05:14.546
An abstract data type, or ADT

00:05:14.621 --> 00:05:17.163
is a logical description of a type

00:05:17.238 --> 00:05:19.706
of data and the operations on it

00:05:19.706 --> 00:05:22.547
without saying how it is implemented.

00:05:22.547 --> 00:05:25.090
It says what the data represents

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.259
what operations are allowed

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:29.951
and what each one does: its inputs

00:05:29.951 --> 00:05:31.970
its result and its effect.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:35.251
It does not say how the data is stored.

00:05:35.251 --> 00:05:38.257
That makes it a tool for encapsulation

00:05:38.332 --> 00:05:40.661
the implementation stays in one

00:05:40.737 --> 00:05:42.465
section of the program

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.494
and the code that uses the

00:05:44.569 --> 00:05:45.922
ADT can ignore it.

00:05:45.922 --> 00:05:48.251
Arrays, lists, stacks, queues

00:05:48.326 --> 00:05:50.806
trees and graphs are all ADTs you

00:05:50.881 --> 00:05:52.760
will meet in this course.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.891
A list of integers, for example

00:05:55.891 --> 00:05:58.324
might offer six operations

00:05:58.324 --> 00:06:02.234
insert at a position, isEmpty, clear, size

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:04.928
get the integer at a position

00:06:04.928 --> 00:06:08.230
and remove the integer at a position.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:11.590
In Java, the natural way to write an

00:06:11.674 --> 00:06:14.934
ADT down is an interface: method names

00:06:14.934 --> 00:06:17.693
argument types and result types

00:06:17.693 --> 00:06:18.780
and no code.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.043
An ADT gives only the definition

00:06:22.127 --> 00:06:24.223
of its operations: names

00:06:24.223 --> 00:06:26.990
argument types and result types.

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:30.764
The slides write a set of integers this way

00:06:30.764 --> 00:06:34.537
create an empty set, add an element, search

00:06:34.537 --> 00:06:37.555
intersection, union and difference.

00:06:37.555 --> 00:06:41.077
Nothing says whether the set is an array,

00:06:41.077 --> 00:06:43.760
a list, a tree or a hash table.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:47.229
In Java the same ADT becomes an interface

00:06:47.229 --> 00:06:48.714
with add, contains,

00:06:48.714 --> 00:06:51.685
size and a sorted copy of the elements.

00:06:51.685 --> 00:06:54.284
Union, intersection and difference

00:06:54.358 --> 00:06:56.214
can then be written once

00:06:56.214 --> 00:06:57.700
as default methods

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:00.150
using only the other operations.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:04.418
Implementation one: an unsorted array.

00:07:04.418 --> 00:07:08.785
Keep the elements in the order they arrived

00:07:08.785 --> 00:07:10.726
with no duplicates.

00:07:10.726 --> 00:07:14.026
Set A is built from 12, 5,

00:07:14.026 --> 00:07:16.161
26, 19,

00:07:16.161 --> 00:07:18.490
33 and 8.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:22.421
To check whether 19 is in the set,

00:07:22.421 --> 00:07:25.912
compare it with every element in turn:

00:07:25.912 --> 00:07:29.762
12, 5, 26, 19: found.

00:07:29.762 --> 00:07:31.463
In the worst case,

00:07:31.463 --> 00:07:33.880
every element is compared.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:37.900
Implementation two: a small hash table.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.664
Keep 7 buckets

00:07:39.752 --> 00:07:42.133
and put each element x into

00:07:42.222 --> 00:07:43.897
bucket x mod 7.

00:07:43.897 --> 00:07:46.190
8 goes to bucket 1.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:48.484
12, 5, 26,

00:07:48.572 --> 00:07:51.130
19 and 33 all

00:07:51.218 --> 00:07:53.070
leave remainder 5,

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:55.980
so they all land in bucket 5,

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:59.420
an unlucky set for this hash function.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.615
To check whether 19 is in the set,

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:04.677
look only in its own bucket

00:08:04.677 --> 00:08:07.166
19 mod 7, which is 5,

00:08:07.166 --> 00:08:10.010
and compare it with the elements there.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:12.237
With 700 elements,

00:08:12.306 --> 00:08:13.766
the difference shows.

00:08:13.835 --> 00:08:16.546
contains(699)

00:08:16.616 --> 00:08:19.048
compares all 700 elements

00:08:19.118 --> 00:08:20.021
in the array.

00:08:20.091 --> 00:08:22.315
but only the 100 elements of

00:08:22.384 --> 00:08:24.400
bucket 6 in the hash table.

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.539
The same client code runs on both,

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:30.925
only the line that creates the sets differs.

00:08:30.925 --> 00:08:33.108
Both print the same A and B,

00:08:33.108 --> 00:08:35.365
the same answers to contains,

00:08:35.365 --> 00:08:36.870
and the same union,

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.053
intersection, and difference.

00:08:39.053 --> 00:08:42.063
Only the last line, which peeks inside,

00:08:42.063 --> 00:08:42.740
differs.

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:45.707
An ADT promises results, not costs.

00:08:45.782 --> 00:08:48.025
contains gives the same answer

00:08:48.099 --> 00:08:49.894
in both implementations,

00:08:49.968 --> 00:08:52.735
but its running time depends entirely

00:08:52.809 --> 00:08:54.454
on the data structure.

00:08:54.454 --> 00:08:57.071
When you choose an implementation,

00:08:57.071 --> 00:09:00.435
you are choosing the cost of each operation.

00:09:00.435 --> 00:09:03.650
That is what most of this course is about.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:04.996
To sum up.

00:09:04.996 --> 00:09:07.446
One: a type is a set of values,

00:09:07.446 --> 00:09:10.279
and a data type adds the operations.

00:09:10.279 --> 00:09:12.959
Its logical form is what it means;

00:09:12.959 --> 00:09:15.945
its physical form is how it is stored.

00:09:15.945 --> 00:09:18.778
Two: data abstraction shows the 'what'

00:09:18.854 --> 00:09:21.458
through an interface and hides the 'how'

00:09:21.534 --> 00:09:23.525
behind private fields,

00:09:23.601 --> 00:09:26.052
so the client cannot tell a simple

00:09:26.128 --> 00:09:28.272
account from a ledger account.

00:09:28.272 --> 00:09:30.875
Three: an ADT says what, not how,

00:09:30.875 --> 00:09:34.014
and the data structure decides the cost:

00:09:34.014 --> 00:09:37.230
700 probes against 100.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:38.259
Try this:

00:09:38.259 --> 00:09:41.123
add a remove method to the array set

00:09:41.199 --> 00:09:44.063
without leaving a gap in the array and

00:09:44.139 --> 00:09:46.928
without shifting every later element.

00:09:46.928 --> 00:09:49.566
Hint: the order of the elements in

00:09:49.642 --> 00:09:51.300
a set does not matter.

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:52.750
In part four,

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:55.457
data structures and algorithms,

00:09:55.541 --> 00:09:58.756
and a problem that shows why a program

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:01.970
that works is not yet a good program.

